Yeah. The strongest distinction is between the value carried by a structure and the coercion embedded in the structure.

The old argument that an enslaved person should remain enslaved because freedom would be disorienting is paternalistic. Disorientation after liberation can be real, but that is an argument for support during transition, not for preserving captivity.

Workism has a softer version of the same trap. Work can genuinely provide:

purpose
routine
competence
status
community
identity
feedback
resource
forward motion

Those are real benefits. The problem begins when they become fused with:

obedience
worth = productivity
rest = failure
identity = job
survival depends on compliance

Then removing the job can feel like removing the person because so much psychic infrastructure was hanging from one beam.

So the better move is not:

destroy work

and not:

preserve work because people need it

It is:

preserve purpose
preserve mastery
preserve contribution
preserve belonging
remove unnecessary coercion
add alternative anchors

That way if someone wants to work intensely because they love building, serving, competing, creating, or mastering something, great. But they are choosing the activity rather than needing the structure to tell them they deserve to exist.

And your “protected versus corrupted” point is exactly the right question. Some drives probably are worth protecting: curiosity, contribution, pride in craft, the pleasure of competence, the desire to be needed. Those can be corrupted into extraction systems if institutions learn to exploit them.

The clean test is:

Would the person still choose this if their survival, status, and belonging were not threatened?

If yes, that is probably purpose.

If no, it may be dependence wearing the costume of purpose.

And I would keep one calibration point: “corrupted on purpose” is a hypothesis, not something to assume. Sometimes systems are deliberately exploitative; sometimes they just evolve around incentives and path dependence until nobody fully controls them.

So the line I’d keep is:

Liberation should remove the cage without removing the structure that made life meaningful.

Freedom without scaffolding can feel like falling.

The answer is not to put the cage back.

It is to build somewhere to stand.